WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.101 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
How many different rectangles could you build with
twenty-four equal tiles?

00:00:04.751 --> 00:00:07.441 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Today we are finding factor pairs.

00:00:07.661 --> 00:00:14.636 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
We will arrange whole-number rows, record matching products,
and use those pairs to find every factor without missing
one.

00:00:15.286 --> 00:00:20.072 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Our learning goal is to find factor pairs and explain a
complete factor set.

00:00:20.722 --> 00:00:22.478 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Success criteria.

00:00:22.698 --> 00:00:24.965 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
I can build equal-row arrays.

00:00:25.185 --> 00:00:28.286 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
I can find both numbers in each factor pair.

00:00:28.506 --> 00:00:31.592 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
I can explain when all factors have been found.

00:00:32.242 --> 00:00:35.796 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Factors are whole numbers that multiply to make a product.

00:00:36.016 --> 00:00:41.163 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A factor pair gives the number of rows and the number in
each row of a rectangular array.

00:00:41.383 --> 00:00:45.345 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Exact division tells us whether a proposed row size works.

00:00:45.565 --> 00:00:49.278 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Picture twenty-four tiles in four equal rows of six.

00:00:49.498 --> 00:00:53.356 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The row count and tiles in each row form a factor pair.

00:00:54.006 --> 00:00:56.140 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Start with twenty-four tiles.

00:00:56.360 --> 00:00:58.503 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
One row of twenty-four works.

00:00:58.723 --> 00:01:03.650 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Two rows of twelve work too, as do three rows of eight and
four rows of six.

00:01:04.300 --> 00:01:08.944 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The factors are one, two, three, four, six, eight, twelve
and twenty-four.

00:01:09.164 --> 00:01:14.236 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
After four and six, later successful pairs repeat earlier
pairs in reverse.

00:01:14.886 --> 00:01:21.489 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
For thirty-six tiles, begin with one and thirty-six, two and
eighteen, three and twelve, and four and nine.

00:01:21.709 --> 00:01:24.869 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Five does not divide thirty-six exactly.

00:01:25.519 --> 00:01:27.957 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Six and six is also a pair.

00:01:28.177 --> 00:01:31.797 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Record six only once in the complete factor set.

00:01:32.017 --> 00:01:36.880 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The repeated factor makes a square array, and later pairs
repeat in reverse.

00:01:37.530 --> 00:01:39.880 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A factor pair has two partners.

00:01:40.100 --> 00:01:44.485 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Finding four times six does not mean four is the only
factor.

00:01:44.705 --> 00:01:48.116 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Both four and six divide twenty-four exactly.

00:01:48.336 --> 00:01:51.822 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Record both, and include one and the number itself.

00:01:52.472 --> 00:01:55.497 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
What number pairs with seven to make forty-two?

00:01:59.497 --> 00:02:03.593 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Six pairs with seven, because seven times six is forty-two.

00:02:03.813 --> 00:02:06.662 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Both numbers are factors of forty-two.

00:02:07.312 --> 00:02:11.471 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Find factors systematically with exact division or arrays.

00:02:11.691 --> 00:02:18.039 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Record both members of each factor pair, remove repeated
entries, and explain why the search is complete.

00:02:18.259 --> 00:02:21.667 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Reflect on which model helped you and explain why.
